## hi-anc | The ancient world | history | 0 | - | hi-time
story 4 | The Great Pyramid was the tallest human-made structure for about 3,800 years
- Built around 2560 BC to about 146 m tall.
- It was overtaken only in the 1300s, by Lincoln Cathedral's central spire (which later collapsed).
- *Its builders were paid workers, not slaves: archaeologists found their villages and bakeries.*
> Cleopatra wasn't Egyptian by descent

myth 4 F | Cleopatra was ethnically Egyptian.
= She was from the Ptolemaic dynasty, descended from one of Alexander the Great's Macedonian Greek generals.
= She was, however, reportedly the first of her dynasty to learn the Egyptian language.
> The Library of Alexandria didn't burn in one night

myth 4 F | The Library of Alexandria was destroyed in a single great fire.
= Caesar's fire in 48 BC damaged part of the collection, but the library seems to have declined over centuries through lost funding and political purges.
= There was no single catastrophic moment.
> Babylonians did algebra 3,800 years ago

story 4 | A 3,800-year-old tablet lists Pythagorean triples
- Plimpton 322, a Babylonian clay tablet from around 1800 BC, lists sets of numbers like 119, 120, 169.
- They satisfy a² + b² = c², over a thousand years before Pythagoras.
- *The Babylonians also computed √2 to about six decimal places.*
> The Antikythera mechanism is a 2,000-year-old computer

story 5! | A shipwreck held an ancient Greek analogue computer
- Divers found corroded bronze gears off the island of Antikythera in 1901.
- X-ray scans revealed a geared device predicting eclipses and planetary positions, built around 100 BC.
- *Nothing comparable is known for over a thousand years afterwards.*
> Next: Rome

## hi-rome | Rome | history | 0 | - | hi-anc
explain 3 | Roman concrete is still standing
- The Pantheon's unreinforced concrete dome, built around 125 AD, is still the largest of its kind.
- Roman marine concrete actually strengthens over time as seawater reacts with volcanic ash.
- *Modern engineers study it to make more durable concrete.*
> The Roman Empire lasted until 1453

quiz 3 | When did the (Eastern) Roman Empire finally fall?
* 1453
o 476
o 410
o 1066
= The Western empire ended in 476. The Eastern (Byzantine) empire lasted until the Ottomans took Constantinople in 1453.
= Its citizens called themselves Romans throughout.
> Caesar's last words weren't "Et tu, Brute?"

myth 3 F | Julius Caesar's last words were "Et tu, Brute?"
= That line comes from Shakespeare.
= Suetonius reports that some said Caesar spoke in Greek, "Kai su, teknon?" ("You too, child?"), but also that he may have said nothing.
> Romans had fast food

real 3 | Pompeii had dozens of street food counters
- Thermopolia were counters with built-in jars serving hot food and drink.
- Many Romans in cramped flats had no kitchen and ate out.
- *A beautifully painted one was excavated in 2020, with duck bones still in the pots.*
> Next: the medieval world

## hi-med | Medieval history | history | 0 | - | hi-rome
myth 4 F | Medieval people thought the Earth was flat.
= Educated medieval Europeans knew Earth was a sphere. Bede, Aquinas and many others say so.
= The myth was largely popularised in the 1800s, including by Washington Irving's romantic biography of Columbus.
> Columbus's real mistake was the Earth's size

explain 4 | Columbus got Earth's size badly wrong
- Scholars of his time correctly estimated Earth's circumference. Columbus used figures making it much smaller.
- He put Japan under 5,000 km west of the Canaries. It's nearly 20,000 km.
- *Without the Americas in the way, his crew would have starved.*
> The Black Death killed a third of Europe

quiz 3 | Roughly what fraction of Europe's population died in the Black Death (1347–1351)?
* Around a third to a half
o About 5%
o About 90%
o About 1%
= Estimates range from 30% to 50% or more in some regions.
= Labour shortages afterwards raised wages for survivors.
> The House of Wisdom kept Greek science alive

story 4 | Baghdad's House of Wisdom
- In the 800s, scholars in Baghdad translated Greek works by Aristotle, Euclid and Ptolemy into Arabic.
- Al-Khwarizmi wrote there; "algebra" comes from his book's title, and "algorithm" from his name.
- *Many Greek works reached medieval Europe only via Arabic translations.*
> Next: the printing press

## hi-print | The printing revolution | history | 0 | - | hi-med
story 4 | Gutenberg's press around 1450 changed everything
- Movable metal type, oil-based ink, and a press adapted from wine presses.
- By 1500, millions of books had been printed in Europe.
- *China had movable type centuries earlier* (Bi Sheng, 1040s), but thousands of characters made it less transformative there.
> The Reformation spread by print

explain 3 | Luther went viral
- 1517: Luther's 95 Theses were printed and spread across Germany within weeks.
- His pamphlets made him one of the best-selling authors of the early 1500s.
- *Without the press, the Reformation might have been one more suppressed protest.*
> Printing fixed spelling, slowly

explain 3 | Printers shaped English spelling
- Early printers sometimes added letters to justify lines and charge by length.
- Many came from Flanders and brought Dutch spellings: "ghost" gained its "h" that way.
- *English spelling froze while pronunciation kept changing* (the Great Vowel Shift).
> Next: the scientific revolution

## hi-sci | The Scientific Revolution | history | 0 | - | hi-print
story 4 | Copernicus published as he died
- Copernicus's sun-centred model was printed in 1543, reportedly reaching him on his deathbed.
- An unsigned preface by the editor, Osiander, called it a mere calculating device.
- *Kepler later exposed who wrote that preface.*
> Galileo's telescope

story 4 | Galileo turned a toy into a revolution
- Telescopes were invented in the Netherlands in 1608. Galileo built better ones in 1609.
- He saw mountains on the Moon, moons around Jupiter and the phases of Venus.
- *Venus's phases were hard evidence that it orbits the Sun.*
> Galileo was pardoned in 1992

quiz 3 | In what year did the Catholic Church formally acknowledge it had erred in condemning Galileo?
* 1992
o 1642
o 1835
o 1965
= Pope John Paul II accepted the findings of a commission in 1992, 359 years after the trial.
> Newton spent more time on alchemy than physics

story 4 | Newton the alchemist
- Newton wrote over a million words on alchemy and theology, far more than on physics.
- He also worked out a date for the end of the world from biblical prophecy: no earlier than 2060.
- *Keynes called him "not the first of the age of reason, but the last of the magicians".*
> The Royal Society's motto

quiz 2 | The Royal Society's motto "Nullius in verba" means…
* Take nobody's word for it
o Knowledge is power
o Through the stars
o Truth conquers all
= Founded 1660. The motto rejects authority in favour of experiment.
> Next: the Industrial Revolution

## hi-ind | The Industrial Revolution | history | 0 | - | hi-sci
explain 3 | Steam engines started as mine pumps
- Newcomen's 1712 engine pumped water out of coal mines. It was very inefficient.
- James Watt's separate condenser (1769) improved efficiency several times over.
- *The watt, the unit of power, is named after him.*
> Railways standardised time

story 4 | Trains forced Britain onto one clock
- Before railways, towns kept local time: Bristol ran about 10 minutes behind London.
- Railways adopted London time in 1840s timetables to avoid confusion and crashes.
- *Legal standard time for Great Britain came in 1880.*
> The Luddites weren't against technology

myth 3 F | The Luddites hated technology itself.
= They were skilled textile workers protesting machines used to cut wages and break labour agreements.
= Their target was how the machines were used, not progress as such.
> Life expectancy at birth was low partly due to infant deaths

explain 4 | Low average life expectancy didn't mean everyone died at 40
- In many historical societies, very high infant and child mortality pulled the average down.
- Adults who survived childhood often lived into their 60s or beyond.
- *Averages can hide very different distributions.*
> Next: the World Wars

## hi-ww1 | The First World War | history | 0 | - | hi-ind
story 4 | A wrong turn started the war
- 28 June 1914: Archduke Franz Ferdinand's driver took a wrong turn in Sarajevo and stalled the car.
- Gavrilo Princip, a member of a group that had failed to kill the Archduke that morning, happened to be nearby.
- *He fired two shots. A month later, Europe was at war.*
> Christmas 1914: an unofficial truce

story 4 | The Christmas truce of 1914
- On parts of the Western Front, British and German soldiers stopped fighting, sang carols and met in no man's land.
- Some swapped cigarettes and souvenirs; there are accounts of football kickabouts.
- *Commanders made sure it didn't happen again.*
> Tanks were codenamed water carriers

quiz 3 | Why were tanks called "tanks"?
* As a secrecy cover: workers were told they were water tanks
o The inventor's name was Tank
o They carried fuel tanks
o From a French word
= Early prototypes were described as water carriers for Mesopotamia. "Tank" stuck.
> Next: the Second World War

## hi-ww2 | The Second World War | history | 0 | - | hi-ww1
story 5 | Bletchley Park read Enigma
- Polish mathematicians first broke Enigma in the 1930s and shared their work with Britain in 1939.
- At Bletchley Park, Turing and others built electromechanical "bombes" to find daily settings.
- *Historians estimate the intelligence shortened the war, possibly by around two years.*
> Colossus was the first programmable electronic digital computer

story 4 | Colossus was kept secret for 30 years
- Built by Tommy Flowers in 1943–44 to break German teleprinter codes (Lorenz, not Enigma).
- Most units were dismantled after the war; its existence wasn't publicly revealed until the 1970s.
- *ENIAC often gets called the first computer because Colossus was secret.*
> Operation Mincemeat fooled Hitler with a corpse

story 5 | Operation Mincemeat
- 1943: British intelligence dressed a dead man as a fictional officer, "Major William Martin", with fake invasion plans.
- His body washed up in Spain. The documents reached German intelligence.
- *Germany reinforced Greece and Sardinia; the Allies invaded Sicily.*
> D-Day weather nearly changed history

explain 4 | D-Day depended on a weather forecast
- Eisenhower delayed the invasion by a day because of storms.
- Group Captain James Stagg's team predicted a short break in the weather for 6 June 1944.
- *German forecasters, lacking Atlantic weather data, expected the storms to continue.*
> Next: the Cold War

## hi-cold | Cold War and space race | history | 0 | - | hi-ww2
story 4 | Sputnik's beep shocked America
- 4 October 1957: the USSR launched Sputnik 1, an 84 kg sphere that beeped on radio.
- Anyone with a shortwave radio could hear it pass over.
- *NASA was founded the next year.*
> The world came closest to nuclear war in 1962

story 5 | Vasili Arkhipov may have prevented nuclear war
- During the Cuban Missile Crisis, a Soviet submarine was being forced to surface by US depth charges.
- Its captain wanted to launch a nuclear torpedo. Launch needed agreement of three senior officers.
- *Arkhipov refused.* The submarine surfaced.
> Apollo 11's computer had less memory than a calculator

real 4 | Apollo's guidance computer
- It had about 4 KB of erasable memory and 72 KB of read-only "core rope" memory, woven by hand.
- During landing it threw "1202" alarms from overload; software designed by Margaret Hamilton's team kept it running.
- *Armstrong landed with well under a minute of hover fuel to spare.*
> The Berlin Wall fell by accident

story 4 | The Berlin Wall opened because of a press conference mistake
- 9 November 1989: official Günter Schabowski announced new travel rules, unprepared.
- Asked when they took effect, he said "immediately, without delay".
- *Crowds gathered at the checkpoints that night; overwhelmed guards opened the gates.*
> Next: history of mathematics

## hi-math | History of mathematics | history | 0 | - | hi-cold
story 4 | Zero arrived late in Europe
- Indian mathematicians treated zero as a number by the 600s (Brahmagupta gave rules for it in 628).
- Fibonacci's 1202 book promoted Hindu-Arabic numerals in Europe.
- *Some Italian cities banned them in official records at first, fearing fraud.*
> Negative numbers were "absurd" for centuries

story 4 | Fermat's last theorem took 358 years
- Around 1637, Fermat scribbled in a margin that xⁿ + yⁿ = zⁿ has no whole-number solutions for n > 2, and that the margin was too small for his proof.
- Andrew Wiles proved it in 1994, using mathematics Fermat couldn't have had.
- *Wiles worked in near-secrecy for about seven years.*
> Euler was the most productive mathematician ever

story 4 | Euler kept working after going blind
- Leonhard Euler lost most of his sight but dictated research to assistants.
- His collected works run to over 80 volumes. About half his output came in the last 17 years of his life.
- *He worked out the orbital calculations in his head.*
> Ramanujan

story 5 | Ramanujan's letter to Hardy
- 1913: a clerk in Madras sent G. H. Hardy pages of extraordinary formulas without proofs.
- Hardy said they "must be true, because, if they were not true, no one would have had the imagination to invent them".
- *Ramanujan came to Cambridge, became a Fellow of the Royal Society, and died at 32.*
> Next: the history of physics

## hi-phys | History of physics | history | 0 | - | hi-math
story 4 | Marie Curie won Nobel Prizes in two sciences
- Physics (1903, shared) for radioactivity and Chemistry (1911) for discovering polonium and radium.
- Her notebooks are still radioactive and kept in lead-lined boxes.
- *She remains the only person to win Nobels in two different sciences.*
> The Solvay conference of 1927

story 4 | 17 of 29 attendees were or became Nobel laureates
- The 1927 Solvay Conference on electrons and photons brought together Einstein, Bohr, Curie, Heisenberg, Schrödinger, Dirac and more.
- Einstein and Bohr argued over quantum mechanics at breakfast and dinner.
- *Einstein: "God does not play dice." Bohr: stop telling God what to do.*
> The Manhattan Project

story 4 | The Manhattan Project employed over 100,000 people
- Most workers had no idea what they were building.
- Trinity, the first test, was on 16 July 1945 in New Mexico.
- Oppenheimer later recalled a line from the Bhagavad Gita: "Now I am become Death, the destroyer of worlds."
> Next: strange history

## hi-odd | Strange but true | history | 0 | - | hi-phys
story 4 | The Great Emu War
- 1932: Australia sent soldiers with machine guns to cull emus destroying crops in Western Australia.
- The emus scattered, dodged and survived in large numbers.
- *The military withdrew. Newspapers declared the emus the winners.*
> The shortest war lasted under an hour

quiz 3 | The Anglo-Zanzibar War of 1896 lasted roughly how long?
* About 40 minutes
o About 3 days
o About a week
o About 6 hours
= Often called the shortest war in recorded history, it ended with Zanzibar's surrender after a short bombardment.
> Napoleon was attacked by rabbits

story 3 | Napoleon was ambushed by rabbits
- According to a popular account, after a 1807 treaty a rabbit hunt was arranged for Napoleon.
- The rabbits, reportedly farm-raised and expecting food, swarmed toward the hunting party instead of fleeing.
- *Napoleon is said to have retreated to his carriage.*
> Ketchup was sold as medicine

story 3 | Ketchup pills
- In the 1830s, tomato extracts were sold in America as cures for indigestion and other ailments.
- Dr John Cook Bennett promoted tomatoes as medicine; tomato pills became a fad.
- *The fad collapsed amid fraudulent imitations.*
> History track complete
